{
 "w": 9,
 "title": "Être and avoir",
 "goal": "By the end of this lesson you can conjugate être (to be) and avoir (to have) in the present, use them in everyday expressions like j'ai faim and j'ai 30 ans, and understand the spoken Quebec forms chu, t'es and t'as.",
 "learn": [
  {
   "h": "Être: to be",
   "body": [
    "*être* is the most important verb in French. It is irregular, so learn it by heart. Use it to say who or what someone is, where they are, and what they are like.",
    "Watch the liaison in *vous‿êtes* [z]. And remember that *c'est* (it is, this is) is simply *ce* + *est*."
   ],
   "table": {
    "head": [
     "Pronoun",
     "être",
     "English"
    ],
    "rows": [
     [
      "je",
      "suis",
      "I am"
     ],
     [
      "tu",
      "es",
      "you are"
     ],
     [
      "il / elle / on",
      "est",
      "he / she is, we are"
     ],
     [
      "nous",
      "sommes",
      "we are"
     ],
     [
      "vous",
      "êtes",
      "you are"
     ],
     [
      "ils / elles",
      "sont",
      "they are"
     ]
    ]
   },
   "tip": "Professions take no article after être: Je suis infirmière (not 'une infirmière')."
  },
  {
   "h": "Avoir: to have",
   "body": [
    "*avoir* means 'to have'. *je* becomes *j'* before it: *j'ai*. Two liaisons matter a lot: *nous‿avons* [z] and *ils‿ont* [z].",
    "Be careful: *ils sont* (they are, with an s sound) and *ils ont* (they have, with a z sound) differ only by that sound."
   ],
   "table": {
    "head": [
     "Pronoun",
     "avoir",
     "English"
    ],
    "rows": [
     [
      "j'",
      "ai",
      "I have"
     ],
     [
      "tu",
      "as",
      "you have"
     ],
     [
      "il / elle / on",
      "a",
      "he / she has, we have"
     ],
     [
      "nous",
      "avons",
      "we have"
     ],
     [
      "vous",
      "avez",
      "you have"
     ],
     [
      "ils / elles",
      "ont",
      "they have"
     ]
    ]
   },
   "tip": "ils sont = eel SÕ (are); ils ont = eel ZÕ (have)."
  },
  {
   "h": "Expressions with avoir where English uses 'be'",
   "body": [
    "French uses *avoir* for age and for many physical feelings. Say *J'ai 30 ans* (I am 30, literally 'I have 30 years'), never *je suis 30*.",
    "Other common ones: *avoir faim* (to be hungry), *avoir soif* (thirsty), *avoir froid* (cold), *avoir chaud* (hot), *avoir peur* (afraid), *avoir raison* (right), *avoir besoin de* (to need)."
   ],
   "table": {
    "head": [
     "French",
     "English"
    ],
    "rows": [
     [
      "J'ai 30 ans.",
      "I'm 30 years old."
     ],
     [
      "J'ai faim.",
      "I'm hungry."
     ],
     [
      "Tu as froid?",
      "Are you cold?"
     ],
     [
      "Il a raison.",
      "He's right."
     ],
     [
      "On a besoin d'un café.",
      "We need a coffee."
     ]
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "h": "C'est and il est",
   "body": [
    "*C'est* (it is, this is, he/she is) is followed by a noun with an article or a name: *C'est un ami. C'est Julie.* It is also used with an adjective to comment on a situation: *C'est bon!*",
    "*Il est / elle est* is followed by an adjective or a profession without article: *Il est grand. Elle est médecin.*"
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Quebec: chu, t'es, t'as",
   "body": [
    "In casual Quebec speech, *je suis* is very often pronounced *chu* (sometimes written *chus* or *j'suis*): *Chu fatigué.* *Tu es* becomes *t'es* and *tu as* becomes *t'as*.",
    "*Il est* often sounds like *y'é* or *y'est*, and *elle est* / *elle a* like *a'l'est* / *a l'a*: *A l'a faim* (she's hungry). Never write these in a formal text, but expect them in every conversation."
   ],
   "table": {
    "head": [
     "Standard",
     "Spoken Quebec"
    ],
    "rows": [
     [
      "je suis",
      "chu"
     ],
     [
      "tu es",
      "t'es"
     ],
     [
      "tu as",
      "t'as"
     ],
     [
      "il est",
      "y'é / y'est"
     ],
     [
      "elle a",
      "a l'a"
     ],
     [
      "ils ont",
      "y'ont"
     ]
    ]
   }
  }
 ],
